      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which basement waterproofing jobs are DIY-friendly?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most of it, yes. Cleaning gutters, extending downspouts, regrading, sealing cracks with an injection kit and coating walls are all reasonable DIY work. Installing an interior perimeter drain is possible but heavy, and exterior excavation is a job for a contractor. Do the cheap outside steps first; they solve many basements on their own and make any later work more effective."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the least expensive fix for a leaky basement?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Managing roof water. Clean gutters and extend every downspout so it discharges several feet from the foundation onto ground that slopes away. That costs roughly $20 to $300 and addresses the most common cause of basement leaks. Fixing grading near the house is the next cheapest step. Both should come before any coating, drain or pump."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does waterproofing paint really work on basement walls?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It works for dampness and slow seepage through the wall when it is applied to bare, clean masonry and brushed into the pores. It does not stop water at the floor joint or a flowing crack, and it can blister if heavy water pressure builds behind it. Use it after outdoor drainage fixes, not as a substitute for them."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is interior or exterior waterproofing better?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Exterior waterproofing is better engineering because the membrane faces the water directly, but it costs far more because it requires excavation. Interior drains with a sump pump are the common compromise: they do not stop water reaching the wall, but they collect it and pump it out. Many homes need neither once gutters, grading and cracks are handled."
          }
