          Waterproofing a basement is less about the product you put on the wall and more about the order you do things in. Start at the roof and work down and outward: move the water away from the house, then close the paths it uses to get in, then manage whatever is left. Skip ahead to coatings or drains and you will pay for a fix that the gutters were quietly undoing.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="outside-in" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why the Work Goes From the Outside In
          </h2>

          <p>
            A foundation sits in soil that holds water. The wetter that soil gets, the more water presses on the walls and slab and the more of it finds a crack, a tie hole or the joint between the wall and floor. Every waterproofing method either keeps the soil around the house drier or deals with the water after it has reached the concrete. The first category is almost always cheaper.
          </p>

          <p>
            Think of it as layers. The outer layer is roof drainage and grading, which decides how much water reaches the foundation at all. The middle layer is the concrete itself: cracks, penetrations and porous block. The inner layer is drainage inside the basement, a perimeter drain and sump pump that collect water and send it back out. Each layer reduces the load on the one inside it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Homeowners usually come at it backward because the symptom is inside. They see a wet wall, buy a coating, and paint it. When the coating blisters, they call a waterproofing company, which sells an interior drain. Sometimes that drain is necessary. Often the house needed a downspout extension and some topsoil. Doing the outside steps first costs little and tells you how much of the inside work you still need.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
