        <div id="trace" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Step 1: Trace the Water Before Spending Anything
          </h2>

          <p>
            Go into the basement during or right after a heavy rain with a flashlight and a notepad. Mark every wet spot with painter tape and write the date. Note whether the water appears on the wall face, along a crack, at the joint where the wall meets the floor, through the floor itself, or around a window, pipe or utility line. Each location points to a different fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rule out condensation with a sheet of plastic taped to the wall for a day or two. Moisture beading on the room side is humid air meeting cool concrete, which a dehumidifier handles. Moisture behind the plastic is coming through the wall. If you only see damp walls in muggy summer weather and never after rain, read our guide on <a href="/how-to-fix-a-damp-basement/">fixing a damp basement</a> before buying anything for the walls.
          </p>

          <p>
            When rain is not cooperating, use a garden hose. Run water at the base of the foundation outside one section at a time for 20 to 30 minutes while someone watches inside. Start low, at the soil line, then move up to the window well and the downspout outlet. When water shows up inside, you have found the path. This is slow, but it beats guessing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay special attention to water at the cove joint, the seam between the bottom of the wall and the edge of the slab. That joint is a cold joint where two separate concrete pours meet, and when the soil around the footing is saturated, water rises through it. No wall coating can stop water there. If that is your leak, the answer is in the outer layer, or in an interior drain.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">MAP IT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Write down where, when and how much.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A sketch of the basement with each wet spot, the date and the weather that day is the single most useful document you can hand a contractor, and it often shows you the fix is outside.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="roof-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Step 2: Get Roof Water Away From the Foundation
          </h2>

          <p>
            A roof sheds an enormous amount of water. An inch of rain on a 1,500-square-foot roof is roughly 900 gallons, and most of it is funneled to a handful of downspouts. If those downspouts empty at the foot of the wall, that is where the soil saturates first and where basement leaks usually start. Fixing this is the cheapest waterproofing you will ever do.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean the gutters and check that they slope toward the downspouts; standing water in a gutter means it overflows along the house. Then extend every downspout so it discharges well away from the foundation. Four feet is a common minimum and six to ten feet is better on clay soils or where the yard slopes back toward the house. Rigid extensions, flexible corrugated pipe and splash blocks all work if the water lands on ground that slopes away.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buried downspout lines are tidy, but they fail quietly. If a downspout feeds an underground pipe, run a hose into it and confirm water exits freely at the other end. Old clay or corrugated lines often crack or clog with roots and silt, and a blocked buried line dumps water right next to the footing, which is the worst possible place for it.
          </p>

          <p>
            After you make these changes, wait for two or three solid rainstorms before doing anything else. Many basements that seemed to need a full system go dry or nearly dry once the roof water is moved. If you still see water, you have lost nothing; you have reduced the load on whatever fix comes next and you will be able to judge it more fairly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="grading" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Step 3: Fix Grading, Window Wells and Hardscape
          </h2>

          <p>
            Soil next to the house should fall away from the foundation. The International Residential Code calls for a drop of at least 6 inches within the first 10 feet where lot conditions allow. Over the years, backfill settles, mulch beds build up and the slope flattens or reverses. A 4-foot level with a scrap board under one end tells you quickly whether the ground runs toward the wall or away from it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Rebuild a low spot with clay-rich topsoil, not sand or mulch, because you want a surface that sheds water rather than soaking it up. Tamp it in layers and keep the finished grade at least 6 inches below siding and wood framing to avoid rot and termite paths. Patios, walkways and driveways that tip toward the house deserve the same scrutiny, because they can deliver water to one spot with great efficiency.
          </p>

          <p>
            Window wells are a common entry point. Check that each well has a drain or a gravel base several inches deep, that the well liner is sealed tight to the foundation, and that the bottom of the well sits a few inches below the window sill. A clear well cover keeps rain and leaves out while letting light in. A well that holds water against a basement window will eventually leak, no matter how good the window is.
          </p>

          <p>
            Look up while you are outside. A tree root heaving a walkway, a sprinkler head spraying the foundation, or an air conditioner condensate line dripping at the wall can all keep one section of soil wet. These are small, cheap fixes that remove a steady water source, and they are easy to miss when you are focused on the visible leak in the basement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cracks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Step 4: Seal Cracks, Tie Holes and Pipe Penetrations
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most poured concrete walls have a few shrinkage cracks. Thin vertical or slightly diagonal cracks are usually not structural, but they are excellent water paths. The standard DIY repair is low-pressure injection: surface ports are glued along the crack, the face is sealed with an epoxy paste, and a polyurethane or epoxy resin is pushed in from the bottom port up until it fills the crack through the full wall thickness.
          </p>

          <p>
            Polyurethane is the usual choice for leaking cracks because it expands and stays somewhat flexible, so it tolerates small seasonal movement. Epoxy is stronger and bonds the two sides of a crack together, which is useful for structural repairs but less forgiving if the crack is still active. Kits for either run roughly $50 to $150 at the time of writing and cover several feet of crack.
          </p>

          <p>
            An actively running leak at a crack or form-tie hole needs hydraulic cement first. It is mixed stiff, pressed into a chiseled-out hole and held for a few minutes while it sets and expands. It stops the flow so you can treat the area properly afterward. Seal pipe and utility penetrations through the wall with a urethane sealant or a foam made for foundations, inside and outside where you can reach.
          </p>

          <p>
            Not every crack is a DIY job. Horizontal cracks, stair-step cracks through block mortar, cracks wider than about a quarter inch, or any wall that bows inward suggest soil pressure on the structure. Have a structural engineer look at those before you seal anything. Sealing a moving crack hides the evidence without addressing the force behind it.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">STRUCTURAL WARNING</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Horizontal cracks are a different problem.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A horizontal crack in a block or poured wall, especially with any inward bow, means the soil is pushing the wall. That calls for an engineer, not a sealant.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="walls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Step 5: Coat the Walls if They Are Still Damp
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once roof water is controlled and cracks are sealed, coat any wall that still looks damp or shows a dark band after rain. For hollow concrete block and for poured walls that seep, a masonry waterproofer brushed onto bare masonry is the usual pick. Our guide to the <a href="/best-basement-wall-sealer/">best basement wall sealers</a> compares the options by the kind of moisture each handles.
          </p>

          <p>
            Preparation is most of the job. Remove paint, efflorescence and dirt with a wire brush and a masonry cleaner, rinse, and let the wall dry. Brush the first coat hard into the pores, working until no pinholes remain, then add a second coat after the label dry time. Block walls in particular need that scrubbing; roll a first coat onto block and you leave pinholes everywhere.
          </p>

          <p>
            Penetrating sealers are an alternative for bare poured concrete. They soak in, react below the surface and leave no film, so there is nothing to peel. They are a good fit for walls you want to leave unfinished. For block foundations, a dedicated <a href="/best-cinder-block-sealer-reviews/">cinder block sealer</a> may be the better match, since the hollow cores and thin shells behave differently from solid concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep expectations honest. Wall coatings reduce dampness and slow seepage. They do not stop water entering at the cove joint, they do not hold back a flowing leak, and they do not fix water rising through the slab. If the coated walls stay dry but water still pools along the floor edge after storms, that is the cue for the next step.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drainage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Step 6: Interior Drain Tile and a Sump Pump
          </h2>

          <p>
            An interior perimeter drain is the standard fix when water keeps entering at the floor joint. A contractor cuts a strip of the slab around the inside of the foundation, digs a trench down beside the footing, lays perforated pipe in washed stone, and slopes it to a sump pit. A dimpled plastic membrane is often run up the wall behind the drain so water weeping through the wall drops into the trench too.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sump pump does the actual work. A 1/3 horsepower submersible pump handles most homes; larger lots with high water tables may need 1/2 horsepower. A cast-iron pump with a mechanical float switch is the durable default. A battery backup pump in the same pit protects you during power outages, which tend to arrive with the same storms that fill the pit. Our ranking of <a href="/best-basement-waterproofing-systems/">basement waterproofing systems</a> compares specific pumps and combos.
          </p>

          <p>
            Interior drain systems are typically $5,000 to $15,000 installed, depending on perimeter length, slab thickness and access. Get at least three quotes, ask each contractor to show you where they think the water is coming from, and ask what warranty covers: the pump, the drain, or dryness. A good contractor will also ask about your gutters and grading before selling you anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            DIY installation is possible for a determined owner with a rented concrete saw, a jackhammer and a strong back, but it is heavy, dusty work and mistakes are hard to undo. A more practical DIY project is replacing or upgrading an existing sump pump and adding a battery backup or a high-water alarm, which takes an afternoon and pays for itself the first time the power goes out in a storm.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Interior Drain Rules of Thumb</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1/3 HP</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical sump pump size</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">10-20 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common discharge distance</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$5k-$15k</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical installed range</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Quotes worth getting</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="excavation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Step 7: When Exterior Excavation Is Worth It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Digging out the foundation and waterproofing it from the outside is the most thorough fix and the most expensive. A crew excavates down to the footing, cleans and repairs the wall, applies a waterproofing membrane, adds drainage board, sets perforated drain pipe at the footing and backfills with free-draining gravel. Water then meets the membrane first and drains away before it ever touches the concrete.
          </p>

          <p>
            It makes the most sense in a few situations. The foundation has structural problems that need exterior repair anyway. The soil around the house stays saturated for long stretches, so interior drainage would run constantly. Or you are building an addition, replacing a sewer line, or otherwise already excavating. In those cases, adding a proper membrane while the wall is exposed is good value.
          </p>

          <p>
            Exterior work typically runs $15,000 to $30,000 or more for a full foundation, with large swings for depth, access, landscaping and decks or patios that must be removed. Partial excavation of one problem wall is far cheaper and sometimes all that is needed, especially when the leak trace from step 1 points clearly at a single side of the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask any contractor which membrane they use and how they handle the footing drain outlet. A footing drain needs somewhere to go, either to daylight on a sloping lot or to a sump. A membrane without a working drain simply holds water against the wall, which defeats the purpose of the excavation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="budget" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Budget and Timeline for a Typical House
          </h2>

          <p>
            For many houses, the full DIY sequence costs under $1,000 and takes a few weekends spread over a month or two, mostly waiting for rain to judge each change. Gutter cleaning and downspout extensions are a Saturday morning. Regrading one side of a house is a weekend with a few yards of topsoil. Crack injection is an afternoon per crack plus cure time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wall coating is the longest DIY step. Plan a day to clear and scrub the walls, a day for patching and drying, then two separate days to apply two coats with dry time between. Work in sections if the basement is in use. Hold off on finishing the basement, adding carpet or framing walls until it has stayed dry through at least one full wet season.
          </p>

          <p>
            Professional steps change the budget by an order of magnitude. That is exactly why the cheap steps come first: they may make the expensive ones unnecessary, and if they do not, they make any system you buy run less and last longer. A sump pump that cycles a few times a storm will outlive one that runs every ten minutes because the downspouts are dumping beside it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep a record of what you did and when, with photos. If you sell the house, buyers and inspectors will ask about water history, and a clear record of drainage fixes, sealed cracks and a dry basement over several seasons is far more reassuring than a freshly painted wall with no explanation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating walls before tracing the leak</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Painting the walls is the most visible fix, so it often comes first. If the water is entering at the floor joint, through the slab or from a downspout dumping beside the wall, the coating does nothing for it. Spend an hour with a hose and a flashlight before buying any product, and fix what the trace shows.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting buried downspout lines</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Underground downspout pipes clog and crack without any visible sign at the surface. When they fail, they deliver roof water straight to the footing. Run a hose into every buried line and confirm it flows freely at the outlet. If it does not, disconnect it and use an above-ground extension until it is repaired.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing a horizontal crack</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Horizontal cracks and inward bowing in a foundation wall are signs of soil pressure, not just a water path. Injecting or patching them hides the evidence while the force keeps working. Have a structural engineer look before you seal, since the right repair might be anchors or bracing rather than resin.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Finishing the basement too soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Drywall, carpet and framing hide moisture and feed mold. Wait until the basement has stayed dry through at least one full wet season after your fixes before finishing it. When you do, use materials that tolerate occasional dampness, such as rigid foam against the wall and moisture-resistant flooring.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the backup pump</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Heavy storms knock out power at the same moment the sump pit fills fastest. A primary pump alone leaves the basement unprotected exactly when it needs help. Add a battery backup pump or at least a high-water alarm, and test both a few times a year by filling the pit with a bucket.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which basement waterproofing jobs are DIY-friendly?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most of it, yes. Cleaning gutters, extending downspouts, regrading, sealing cracks with an injection kit and coating walls are all reasonable DIY work. Installing an interior perimeter drain is possible but heavy, and exterior excavation is a job for a contractor. Do the cheap outside steps first; they solve many basements on their own and make any later work more effective.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the least expensive fix for a leaky basement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Managing roof water. Clean gutters and extend every downspout so it discharges several feet from the foundation onto ground that slopes away. That costs roughly $20 to $300 and addresses the most common cause of basement leaks. Fixing grading near the house is the next cheapest step. Both should come before any coating, drain or pump.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does waterproofing paint really work on basement walls?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It works for dampness and slow seepage through the wall when it is applied to bare, clean masonry and brushed into the pores. It does not stop water at the floor joint or a flowing crack, and it can blister if heavy water pressure builds behind it. Use it after outdoor drainage fixes, not as a substitute for them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is interior or exterior waterproofing better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Exterior waterproofing is better engineering because the membrane faces the water directly, but it costs far more because it requires excavation. Interior drains with a sump pump are the common compromise: they do not stop water reaching the wall, but they collect it and pump it out. Many homes need neither once gutters, grading and cracks are handled.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many years do these fixes hold up?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Gutter and grading fixes last as long as you maintain them. Polyurethane crack injections often last decades. Masonry coatings typically go five to ten years before touch-ups. Interior drain systems can last many decades, though sump pumps usually need replacing every 7 to 10 years. Exterior membranes, installed well, are often expected to last as long as the foundation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a permit to waterproof my basement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Gutter work, grading, crack sealing and coatings usually do not need a permit. Interior drain installation, sump pump discharge lines and excavation sometimes do, depending on your municipality, especially if discharge connects to a storm sewer or crosses a property line. Check with your local building department, and ask any contractor whether they pull permits.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my basement leak is serious?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Treat it as serious if water pools on the floor after storms, if a crack is horizontal or wider than about a quarter inch, if a wall bows inward, or if the leak is growing season to season. Damp spots, light efflorescence and condensation are lower priority. When structure is in question, a structural engineer is a better first call than a waterproofing salesperson.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

