        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Waterproof a basement in the order water arrives: roof, soil, concrete, then the basement itself. Each step is cheaper than the one after it, and each makes the next one smaller or unnecessary. Many basements are dry after the first two steps.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If water still gets in after gutters, grading and crack repair, coat damp walls with a proper masonry product and, for water at the floor joint, get quotes for an interior drain with a battery-backed sump pump. Keep notes and photos as you go, and wait for a full wet season before finishing the space.
          </p>
